WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.578
Mechanical ventilation is the largest topic on the study guide, so it is split into four parts.

2
00:00:08.578 --> 00:00:11.384
This first part covers the modes.

3
00:00:11.384 --> 00:00:15.295
We start with how to tell any two modes apart.

4
00:00:15.295 --> 00:00:27.114
Then the core pairs: volume control versus pressure control, control mode versus assist control, and IMV versus SIMV with pressure support.

5
00:00:27.114 --> 00:00:40.974
Then the advanced modes: minute ventilation guarantees, PRVC, Volume Ventilation Plus, proportional assist, airway pressure release and high-frequency oscillation.

6
00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:44.375
We finish with all of them side by side.

7
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:54.862
Mode names depend on the manufacturer, so the deck says learn how the mode works, not just the name.

8
00:00:54.862 --> 00:01:05.102
The same idea is PRVC on a Servo, assist control with AutoFlow on a Dräger, and VC plus on a Puritan Bennett 840.

9
00:01:05.102 --> 00:01:06.915
Ask three questions.

10
00:01:06.915 --> 00:01:10.449
What is controlled, volume or pressure?

11
00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:15.795
What starts and ends the breath, the trigger and the cycle?

12
00:01:15.795 --> 00:01:18.423
And which breath types occur?

13
00:01:18.423 --> 00:01:25.764
The golden rule is that you control only one item, and the other is the variable.

14
00:01:25.764 --> 00:01:33.466
Hess says the ventilator can control only one variable at a time, pressure or volume.

15
00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:44.975
The deck ranks them: volume control first, pressure control second, and pressure support as help, in SIMV and breathing trials.

16
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.905
A spontaneous breath is triggered and cycled by the patient.

17
00:01:51.905 --> 00:01:57.135
A mandatory breath is triggered or cycled by the machine.

18
00:01:57.135 --> 00:02:02.731
So a patient-triggered breath is not necessarily spontaneous.

19
00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:05.759
There are three breath sequences.

20
00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:11.081
Continuous mandatory ventilation is all mandatory breaths.

21
00:02:11.081 --> 00:02:17.962
Intermittent mandatory ventilation mixes mandatory and spontaneous breaths.

22
00:02:17.962 --> 00:02:25.944
Continuous spontaneous ventilation is all spontaneous, as in CPAP and pressure support.

23
00:02:25.944 --> 00:02:31.266
Open-loop modes deliver what you set, like volume control.

24
00:02:31.266 --> 00:02:39.248
Closed-loop modes adjust to the patient, like pressure support, MMV, ASV, PAV and PRVC.

25
00:02:39.248 --> 00:02:52.735
For the NBRC, combine the control variable, volume or pressure, with assist control, machine breaths only, or SIMV, which adds spontaneous breaths.

26
00:02:52.735 --> 00:02:59.800
That gives VC-A/C, VC-SIMV, PC-A/C and PC-SIMV, and the deck says know these.

27
00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:08.569
Volume control guarantees the tidal volume, and Hess adds the flow, while the pressure varies.

28
00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:14.733
Pressure control guarantees the pressure, while the tidal volume and flow vary.

29
00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:23.317
If the patient develops ARDS on volume control, the tidal volume is still five hundred and the pressure rises.

30
00:03:23.317 --> 00:03:29.326
On pressure control, the pressure is still twenty and the tidal volume falls.

31
00:03:29.326 --> 00:03:37.754
A volume control breath is volume cycled with constant, square flow, and its pressure looks like a mountain.

32
00:03:37.754 --> 00:03:50.083
A pressure control breath is time triggered or patient triggered, pressure limited and time cycled, with descending flow, and its pressure looks like a table.

33
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:58.109
Use volume control when a constant tidal volume is needed to hold a desired PaCO2.

34
00:03:58.109 --> 00:04:10.280
Its weakness is that plateau pressure rises as compliance falls, and the fixed flow can mismatch the patient's demand, especially if it is set too low.

35
00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:24.788
Use pressure control in severe ARDS, where volume control pressures of fifty or more can cause barotrauma; pressure control gives a lower peak pressure, and the patient is sedated.

36
00:04:24.788 --> 00:04:33.896
Its strengths are that alveolar pressure cannot exceed the set pressure, and the variable flow improves matching.

37
00:04:33.896 --> 00:04:40.425
Its weakness is that tidal volume changes with compliance, resistance and effort.

38
00:04:40.425 --> 00:04:48.002
Peak pressure in pressure control is the set pressure plus PEEP, so twenty plus ten is thirty.

39
00:04:48.002 --> 00:04:59.850
The chapter eleven deck adds that if volume control needs a plateau above thirty, you should probably switch to pressure control at thirty or less.

40
00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:07.544
The decks use CMV two ways, so decide by whether the patient can trigger.

41
00:05:07.544 --> 00:05:18.121
In the Chang deck, CMV is the control mode: a preset tidal volume at a time-triggered rate, and the patient cannot trigger.

42
00:05:18.121 --> 00:05:23.452
Assist control allows patient-triggered breaths; CMV does not.

43
00:05:23.452 --> 00:05:31.535
CMV needs sedation, with or without paralysis, because an awake patient fights the ventilator.

44
00:05:31.535 --> 00:05:39.790
It is used for tetanus or seizures, complete rest, a crushed chest and a paradoxical chest wall.

45
00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:47.357
Its risks are prolonged sedation, apnea and hypoxia if disconnected, and disuse atrophy.

46
00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:57.074
In assist control, assist is patient triggered and control is time triggered, and every breath gets full support.

47
00:05:57.074 --> 00:06:07.478
Set twelve, the patient triggers twenty four, and you get twenty four full breaths, with a risk of respiratory alkalosis.

48
00:06:07.478 --> 00:06:13.842
Hess calls CMV the same as assist control, with the set rate as a minimum.

49
00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:22.376
IMV gives mandatory breaths at a fixed interval, with spontaneous breaths in between.

50
00:06:22.376 --> 00:06:38.102
Its problem is breath stacking: a mandatory breath arrives before the patient finishes exhaling, causing auto-PEEP, hyperinflation, higher pressures, more work of breathing and barotrauma.

51
00:06:38.102 --> 00:06:40.946
No new adult ventilators offer it.

52
00:06:40.946 --> 00:06:43.706
SIMV is IMV with synchronization.

53
00:06:43.706 --> 00:06:52.823
The ventilator waits in a synchronization window and delivers the mandatory breath with the patient's effort.

54
00:06:52.823 --> 00:07:07.545
On SIMV ten, a mandatory breath comes every six seconds, and in between the patient breathes any tidal volume, with pressure support applying only to those spontaneous breaths.

55
00:07:07.545 --> 00:07:22.852
Compare it with assist control: at a rate of ten with the patient triggering twenty, assist control gives twenty mandatory breaths, while SIMV gives ten mandatory and ten spontaneous.

56
00:07:22.852 --> 00:07:26.867
The instructor's word play says SIMV equals IMV.

57
00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:41.631
SIMV is used for partial support, to preserve muscle strength, and for weaning, stepping down from twelve to eight to four and then to CPAP or pressure support.

58
00:07:41.631 --> 00:07:50.986
Without pressure support, the patient tires and weaning fails, so the combination is SIMV plus pressure support.

59
00:07:50.986 --> 00:07:59.840
Hess warns of asynchrony if the rate is set too low, and the mandatory rate can never exceed the set rate.

60
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.854
Pressure support is patient triggered, pressure limited and flow cycled.

61
00:08:05.854 --> 00:08:21.306
It works only on spontaneous breaths, its tidal volume depends on effort, compliance and resistance, and it cycles off at a set percentage of peak flow, often about twenty five percent.

62
00:08:21.306 --> 00:08:24.563
It cannot be used in an apneic patient.

63
00:08:24.563 --> 00:08:33.167
It is indicated for a low spontaneous tidal volume, a high spontaneous rate, or more work of breathing.

64
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:45.935
Titrate pressure support to a tidal volume of eight to ten milliliters per kilogram of ideal body weight, and a spontaneous rate below twenty five.

65
00:08:45.935 --> 00:08:56.962
The notes call that tidal volume the historical figure, and modern lung-protective practice targets closer to six to eight, especially in ARDS.

66
00:08:56.962 --> 00:09:00.201
The deck's example is a five foot ten man.

67
00:09:00.201 --> 00:09:12.924
Ideal body weight is fifty plus two point three times ten, or seventy three kilograms, so the target is five hundred eighty four to seven hundred thirty milliliters.

68
00:09:12.924 --> 00:09:22.100
Hess adds that too little pressure support causes fatigue and tachypnea, and too much activates the expiratory muscles.

69
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.302
Pause and work it out.

70
00:09:25.302 --> 00:09:31.034
The set rate is ten, and the patient triggers twenty breaths a minute.

71
00:09:31.034 --> 00:09:41.025
How many mandatory breaths does the patient get on assist control, and how many mandatory and spontaneous breaths on SIMV?

72
00:09:41.933 --> 00:09:47.933
[6-second practice pause]

73
00:09:48.433 --> 00:10:00.281
On assist control, every triggered breath is a full mandatory breath, so the patient gets twenty mandatory breaths, which risks respiratory alkalosis.

74
00:10:00.281 --> 00:10:15.683
On SIMV, only the set ten are mandatory, synchronized with the patient's effort, and the other ten are spontaneous, at whatever tidal volume the patient makes, with pressure support if it is set.

75
00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:23.666
Mandatory minute ventilation guarantees a preset minimum minute ventilation.

76
00:10:23.666 --> 00:10:34.380
If the spontaneous minute ventilation falls below it, the ventilator automatically raises the mandatory, volume-cycled rate.

77
00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.836
Say the target is eight liters a minute.

78
00:10:37.836 --> 00:10:41.637
If the patient makes nine, nothing is added.

79
00:10:41.637 --> 00:10:46.821
If the patient drops to six, mandatory breaths fill the gap.

80
00:10:46.821 --> 00:10:55.116
The catch is that it does not guarantee an adequate tidal volume on each breath, only the total.

81
00:10:55.116 --> 00:11:11.186
It is used for weaning, a transition from full support, and variable drive, such as neuromuscular disease or coming off sedation, and the notes add that it needs reliable patient effort.

82
00:11:11.186 --> 00:11:19.221
Contrast it with ASV, which picks the rate and tidal volume, and PAV, which amplifies effort.

83
00:11:19.221 --> 00:11:26.911
Hess and the Principles deck instead call it an IMV type where the set rate is a maximum.

84
00:11:26.911 --> 00:11:28.725
Use the RCP 202 deck.

85
00:11:30.133 --> 00:11:34.049
Pressure-regulated volume control is a hybrid.

86
00:11:34.049 --> 00:11:46.305
It gives pressure-controlled breaths and adjusts the pressure automatically to reach a target tidal volume at the lowest possible peak pressure.

87
00:11:46.305 --> 00:11:54.647
The clinician sets the rate, tidal volume, FiO2 and PEEP, and the ventilator adjusts the pressure.

88
00:11:54.647 --> 00:12:01.796
If compliance worsens, it raises the pressure; if compliance improves, it lowers it.

89
00:12:01.796 --> 00:12:09.882
Use it when you want volume control's volume guarantee with pressure control's lower pressures.

90
00:12:09.882 --> 00:12:19.586
The board pearl: the mode that adjusts pressure to a target tidal volume while minimizing airway pressure is PRVC.

91
00:12:19.586 --> 00:12:25.203
It is also called VC plus, AutoFlow, or adaptive pressure control.

92
00:12:25.203 --> 00:12:37.971
Hess cautions that if demand rises, from pain for example, and the tidal volume exceeds the target, support is taken away, a cause of mode asynchrony.

93
00:12:37.971 --> 00:12:50.908
On the Siemens 300A, automode switches to PRVC after no trigger for twelve seconds in adults, and to volume support after two patient-triggered breaths.

94
00:12:52.333 --> 00:13:00.039
Volume Ventilation Plus, on the Puritan Bennett 840, combines VC plus and volume support.

95
00:13:00.039 --> 00:13:05.666
VC plus handles the mandatory breaths, in assist control or SIMV.

96
00:13:05.666 --> 00:13:24.281
It is Puritan Bennett's version of PRVC: preset tidal volume and inspiratory time, variable pressure and flow, and a test breath to measure compliance, with cough pressure vented through the active exhalation valve.

97
00:13:24.281 --> 00:13:32.939
Volume support handles the spontaneous breaths: it guarantees the volume and lets the pressure vary.

98
00:13:32.939 --> 00:13:35.969
You set only a target tidal volume.

99
00:13:35.969 --> 00:13:41.683
Less effort gets more pressure support, and more effort gets less.

100
00:13:41.683 --> 00:13:45.839
It is commonly used for weaning from anesthesia.

101
00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:57.527
The contrast: pressure support guarantees pressure and lets volume vary, while volume support guarantees volume and lets pressure vary.

102
00:13:57.527 --> 00:14:02.808
Both are patient triggered, pressure limited and flow cycled.

103
00:14:04.233 --> 00:14:10.776
With proportional assist ventilation, there is no target flow, volume or pressure.

104
00:14:10.776 --> 00:14:16.680
Support is proportional to the patient's elastance, resistance and demand.

105
00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:22.185
Pull harder and you get more assist; make no effort and you get none.

106
00:14:22.185 --> 00:14:27.771
The clinician sets a percent assist, such as fifty or seventy percent.

107
00:14:27.771 --> 00:14:39.260
The Chang notes say it is set to overcome eighty percent of the elastance and resistance, and Hess gives a range of five to ninety five percent.

108
00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:53.701
It suits spontaneously breathing patients who need variable assist, such as COPD, neuromuscular disease and coming off sedation, and weaning, with the advantage of better synchrony.

109
00:14:53.701 --> 00:15:00.244
It is not for apneic patients, leaks reduce its accuracy, and overshoot can occur.

110
00:15:00.244 --> 00:15:11.733
Hess adds that it is flow cycled, that too high a percent causes runaway, and that it has no minimum support level, so alarms and backup matter.

111
00:15:13.133 --> 00:15:26.523
The deck defines airway pressure release ventilation as inverse ratio, pressure controlled, intermittent mandatory ventilation with unrestricted spontaneous breathing.

112
00:15:26.523 --> 00:15:32.777
You set the FiO2, a high pressure, a low pressure, a time high and a time low.

113
00:15:32.777 --> 00:15:53.784
The chapter eleven starting values are a low pressure of zero to eight, a high pressure that gives four to eight milliliters per kilogram, kept below thirty to thirty five, a time high of three to five seconds, and a time low of point two to point eight seconds.

114
00:15:53.784 --> 00:16:01.562
Most of the time is spent at the high pressure, with less than one and a half seconds at the low.

115
00:16:01.562 --> 00:16:10.382
The release to the low pressure removes carbon dioxide, and the patient breathes spontaneously at both levels.

116
00:16:10.382 --> 00:16:15.433
It is used for hypoxemic failure and ARDS with an intact drive.

117
00:16:15.433 --> 00:16:20.083
To wean, lower the release frequency; at zero, it is CPAP.

118
00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:34.099
High-frequency oscillatory ventilation uses a tiny tidal volume, around or below the anatomic dead space, at a very fast rate, to minimize lung injury.

119
00:16:34.099 --> 00:16:41.191
You set the amplitude, the frequency in hertz, the mean airway pressure and the FiO2.

120
00:16:41.191 --> 00:16:57.627
To improve ventilation and lower carbon dioxide, raise the amplitude or lower the frequency, which is backwards from conventional ventilation, because a lower frequency gives a larger tidal volume.

121
00:16:57.627 --> 00:17:03.218
To improve oxygenation, raise the mean airway pressure or the FiO2.

122
00:17:03.218 --> 00:17:14.648
Chapter eleven uses it for hypoxemic failure not responding to conventional methods, severe infant respiratory distress syndrome or ARDS.

123
00:17:14.648 --> 00:17:20.989
But Hess strongly recommends against routine use in moderate or severe ARDS.

124
00:17:20.989 --> 00:17:36.675
To wean an adult, lower the FiO2 to point four zero first, then the mean airway pressure by two to three every four to six hours until it is twenty or less, then consider pressure control.

125
00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:39.493
Pause and think.

126
00:17:39.493 --> 00:17:44.632
A patient on high-frequency oscillation has a rising PaCO2.

127
00:17:44.632 --> 00:17:50.902
Which two settings could you change to lower it, and in which direction?

128
00:17:50.902 --> 00:17:56.650
Watch out, one of them is backwards from conventional ventilation.

129
00:17:57.567 --> 00:18:03.567
[6-second practice pause]

130
00:18:04.067 --> 00:18:08.168
Raise the amplitude, or lower the frequency.

131
00:18:08.168 --> 00:18:22.338
Lowering the frequency is the backwards one: with fewer oscillations per second, each one moves a larger tidal volume, which clears more carbon dioxide.

132
00:18:22.338 --> 00:18:30.542
Raising the mean airway pressure or the FiO2 would help oxygenation, not carbon dioxide.

133
00:18:31.967 --> 00:18:34.898
Here are the modes side by side.

134
00:18:34.898 --> 00:18:47.358
Volume control guarantees tidal volume and is the first choice; pressure control guarantees pressure and is the second, for severe ARDS.

135
00:18:47.358 --> 00:18:58.993
Control mode, in the Chang sense, allows no patient trigger and needs sedation; assist control gives every breath full support.

136
00:18:58.993 --> 00:19:11.727
IMV is historical, with breath stacking; SIMV synchronizes mandatory breaths and adds spontaneous ones, with pressure support, for weaning.

137
00:19:11.727 --> 00:19:15.758
MMV guarantees a minimum minute ventilation.

138
00:19:15.758 --> 00:19:20.797
PRVC hits a target tidal volume at the lowest pressure.

139
00:19:20.797 --> 00:19:24.645
VV plus pairs VC plus with volume support.

140
00:19:24.645 --> 00:19:30.417
PAV has no target, only a percent assist, and is not for apnea.

141
00:19:30.417 --> 00:19:36.738
APRV sets two pressures and two times, for ARDS with an intact drive.

142
00:19:36.738 --> 00:19:45.442
HFOV oscillates tiny volumes, for refractory hypoxemia and neonates, but not routinely in ARDS.

143
00:19:46.867 --> 00:19:49.091
Here is part one in brief.

144
00:19:49.091 --> 00:20:00.896
Ask what is controlled, what triggers and cycles the breath, and which breath types occur, and remember you can control only one variable.

145
00:20:00.896 --> 00:20:11.418
Volume control fixes the tidal volume and lets pressure rise; pressure control fixes the pressure and lets the volume fall.

146
00:20:11.418 --> 00:20:23.993
Assist control gives every triggered breath full support; SIMV gives only the set breaths and leaves the rest spontaneous, so add pressure support.

147
00:20:23.993 --> 00:20:27.500
PRVC adjusts pressure to a target volume.

148
00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:37.851
APRV and HFOV are rescue options for the sickest lungs, and HFOV is not recommended routinely in moderate or severe ARDS.

149
00:20:37.851 --> 00:20:42.642
Part two covers asynchrony and the ventilator variables.
